# rc2/crypto-bench (2026-11-01): the AES implementations a benchmark has to be able to compare.
#
# This device has exactly one AES implementation today: the crypto API's `aes` cipher, driver name `aes-lib`
# (crypto/aes.c, backed on ARM by the scalar asm core in lib/crypto/arm/aes-cipher-core.S), priority 100. This turns on
# the bit-sliced NEON one (aes-neonbs, priority 250) -- the "just use the CPU" answer to the crypto engine, and the
# thing the round measures against the hardware. It is also the only way `ctr(aes)` exists here at all: `aes-lib` is a
# cipher, and only the neonbs glue registers the ctr template over it. Nothing here changes a default path beyond that
# priority: both register under the same algorithm names.
#
# CONFIG_CRYPTO_MANAGER_EXTRA_TESTS stays off. rc2-cryptobench runs its own known-answer tests per implementation
# (tools/mainline-rc2/cryptobench/), and a boot-time sweep would only turn a broken engine into a boot that looks
# broken.
CONFIG_CRYPTO_AES_ARM_BS=y
